; `vecTry`, the reader for an index nobody has checked.
;
; THIS FILE'S PREMISE WAS ANSWERED, AND THE HEADER SAYS SO RATHER THAN
; BEING QUIETLY REWRITTEN. It used to open: "`vecGet` answers 0 for an
; index past the end. 0 IS ALSO A VALUE A CALLER MAY HAVE PUSHED, so
; the two cases arrive identically and nothing in the answer says which
; happened. That is not a crash - it is a wrong answer that keeps
; going, which on a parser fed by a peer is the worse of the two
; failure modes."
;
; That argument was accepted. `vecGet` REFUSES an out-of-range index
; now - status 77, `docs/generics-design.md` section 4 - so the wrong
; answer that keeps going is gone, and with it the term this file was
; built around: a stored zero and a missing element can no longer be
; COMPARED in one program, because reading the missing one ends it.
; The refusal is pinned by `tests/stdlib/464-index-trap.ax` and its
; `.exit`, which is the only shape a fixture for a trap can take.
;
; THE LOAD-BEARING TERM IS STILL THE DISCRIMINATION ONE. Any single
; assertion here can be satisfied by an accessor that is wrong in an
; interesting way: one that always answers `None` passes every
; out-of-range term, one that always answers `Some` passes every
; in-range term. Both halves are still asserted on the same vector -
; `Some 0` for the stored zero, `None` for the element past the end.
;
; `vecGet` is still the right call where the index is known good, and
; that has not changed: what changed is what it does when the index is
; NOT good. This is not a deprecation; it is the second accessor for
; the case the first now refuses.
(import IO)

(import Vec)

(:: say (-> Int String Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (say okFlag label)
  (if (== okFlag 1)
    (printlnLit (strCStr (strConcat "ok   " label)))
    (printlnLit (strCStr (strConcat "FAIL " label)))
  )
)

(:: isNone (-> (Option Int) Int))

(fn (isNone o)
  (match o
    ((Some x) 0)
    ((None) 1)
  )
)

(:: someIs (-> (Option Int) Int Int))

(fn (someIs o want)
  (match o
    ((Some x)
      (if (== x want)
        1
        0
      )
    )
    ((None) 0)
  )
)

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  (let ((v vecNew))
    {
      (say (isNone (vecTry v 0)) "an empty vector has nothing at 0")
      (say (isNone (vecTry v (- 0 1))) "a negative index is nothing")
      (vecPush v 0)
      (vecPush v 7)
      (say (someIs (vecTry v 0) 0) "a stored zero reads back as Some 0")
      (say (someIs (vecTry v 1) 7) "a stored value reads back")
      (say (isNone (vecTry v 2)) "one past the end is nothing")
      (say (isNone (vecTry v 99)) "far past the end is nothing")
      (say (isNone (vecTry v (- 0 1))) "a negative index is still nothing once there are elements")
      ; THE TERM THIS FILE EXISTS FOR, and it has been ANSWERED.
      ;
      ; Element 0 holds a stored zero and element 2 does not exist.
      ; This file used to assert that `vecGet` answers 0 for BOTH -
      ; "asserted here, not assumed, because if `vecGet` ever started
      ; distinguishing them this file should say so rather than quietly
      ; keep passing". It started distinguishing them, so this says so.
      ;
      ; `vecGet` REFUSES a missing element now, status 77
      ; (`docs/generics-design.md` section 4), so the pair cannot be
      ; compared in one program any more - a trap ends it. The refusal
      ; is pinned by `tests/stdlib/464-index-trap.ax` and its `.exit`,
      ; which is the only shape a fixture for a trap can take.
      ;
      ; What remains here is the half that was always the point:
      ; `vecTry` tells the two apart, and it is now the only reader
      ; that can be asked about an index nobody has checked.
      (say
        (someIs (vecTry v 0) 0)        "the stored zero is Some 0, not an absence"
      )
      (say
        (if (== (someIs (vecTry v 0) 0) 1)
          (if (== (isNone (vecTry v 2)) 1)
            1
            0
          )
          0
        )        "vecTry tells those two apart"
      )
      ; And it keeps up as the vector grows, so the bound is read rather
      ; than remembered from when the accessor was written.
      (vecPush v 9)
      (say (someIs (vecTry v 2) 9) "what was past the end is readable once pushed")
      (say (isNone (vecTry v 3)) "the end moved with it")
      0
    }
  )
)
